findings.description:
devnet_roundtrip.py:45-48 reads `SOLANA_KEYPAIR_PATH` (default `~/.config/solana/id.json`), runs `Path(keypair_path).expanduser()`, and opens the file for reading with no:
- check that the resolved path stays within an expected directory (no path-traversal guard);
- check that file mode is 0600 (Solana CLI itself warns on world-readable keypairs);
- check that the file is actually a 64-byte secret key array (a malformed/attacker-supplied file could cause solana-py to misbehave).
Also, the keypair is loaded inside the `sign_attestation` function which returns only the public key — the secret bytes remain in process memory longer than necessary with no scrubbing.

arweave_roundtrip.py performs `requests.post(f"{ARWEAVE_RPC_URL}/tx", ...)` and `requests.get(f"{ARWEAVE_RPC_URL}/tx/{tx_id}")` and `.../data` using a URL composed by string concatenation from `os.getenv('ARWEAVE_RPC_URL', ...)`. devnet_roundtrip.py does the same with `Client(SOLANA_RPC_URL)`. There is:
- no scheme validation (an attacker setting `ARWEAVE_RPC_URL=file:///etc/passwd` or `http://169.254.169.254/...` is accepted);
- no host allowlist (link-local / RFC1918 / cloud-metadata endpoints reachable from the runner);
- `requests.get` calls on lines 79 and 102 have NO `timeout=` argument (only the POST on line 52 does), so a slow-loris server can hang CI indefinitely.
